**Key idea:** four inputs can be sorted with five compare-and-swap (CAS) units in three parallel layers. Specify whether the key is the complete 1024-bit word or a field inside a record.

| Layer | CAS pairs | Example values after layer |
|---|---|---|
| Input | — | 9, 2, 7, 1 |
| 1 | (0,1), (2,3) | 2, 9, 1, 7 |
| 2 | (0,2), (1,3) | 1, 7, 2, 9 |
| 3 | (1,2) | 1, 2, 7, 9 |

### Complete unsigned combinational implementation
```systemverilog
module sort4(input logic [1023:0] x[0:3],
             output logic [1023:0] y[0:3]);
  logic [1023:0] a[0:3], b[0:3];
  task automatic cas(input logic [1023:0] p,q,
                     output logic [1023:0] lo,hi);
    if (p <= q) begin lo=p; hi=q; end
    else begin lo=q; hi=p; end
  endtask
  always_comb begin
    cas(x[0],x[1],a[0],a[1]);
    cas(x[2],x[3],a[2],a[3]);
    cas(a[0],a[2],b[0],b[2]);
    cas(a[1],a[3],b[1],b[3]);
    y[0]=b[0]; y[3]=b[3];
    cas(b[1],b[2],y[1],y[2]);
  end
endmodule
```
### Pitfalls and follow-up
A 1024-bit comparator and two wide muxes can dominate timing. Register each layer when throughput matters, carrying valid alongside the data. Equal-key stability is not guaranteed merely by avoiding swaps on equality in each CAS; compare `(key, original_index)` when stable order is required. Check sortedness and preservation of the input multiset, including equal values and maximum-width operands.

