> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** the time unit scales numeric delays, while time precision determines their rounding resolution. `1ns/1ps` means one delay unit is one nanosecond and local delays resolve to picoseconds.

| Delay expression | With 1 ns unit, 1 ps precision |
|---|---|
| #1 | 1 ns |
| #1.25 | 1.25 ns |
| #0.0004 | Rounds to 0 ps |
| #0.001 | 1 ps |

```systemverilog
module timing_demo;
  timeunit 1ns;
  timeprecision 1ps;
  logic clk=0;
  always #5ns clk=~clk;
  initial begin
    #1.25ns;
    $display("elapsed_ns=%0.3f", $realtime);
    #18.75ns;
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```
### Explanation
The example has a ten-nanosecond clock period, or 100 MHz. The explicit `timeunit` declaration scopes the choice to the design unit instead of relying on the last textual timescale directive seen during compilation. Explicit delay suffixes improve readability, but delays are still quantized by the applicable precision.

### Pitfalls and follow-up
A requested nonzero delay that rounds to zero can create a zero-time loop in a clock generator. Use sufficient precision and a strictly positive resolved half-period. Integer `time` and real-valued `$realtime` differ when representing fractional local time units. Finer precision may increase event-processing cost; it does not make the behavioral model physically more accurate or automatically alter a separately specified clock period.

