**Key idea:** construct the mask in the same unsigned width as the operand and reject out-of-range bit indices before shifting.

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <limits>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cassert>

std::uint64_t set_bit(std::uint64_t x, unsigned n) {
    if (n >= std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::digits)
        throw std::out_of_range("bit index");
    return x | (std::uint64_t{1} << n);
}
std::uint64_t clear_bit(std::uint64_t x, unsigned n) {
    if (n >= std::numeric_limits<std::uint64_t>::digits)
        throw std::out_of_range("bit index");
    return x & ~(std::uint64_t{1} << n);
}
int main() {
    assert(set_bit(0,63) == UINT64_C(0x8000000000000000));
    assert(clear_bit(0xff,3) == 0xf7);
}
```

| Operation on 0010 | Mask | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Set bit 0 | 0001 | 0011 |
| Clear bit 1 | 0010, complemented for AND | 0000 |

### Explanation
OR with the mask forces the selected bit high while preserving all others. AND with its complement forces that bit low. Both operations are idempotent. Bit zero is the least significant bit; this numerical convention is independent of memory endianness.

### Pitfalls and follow-up
Plain `1 << n` uses an int and is unsuitable for high positions in a 64-bit word. Shifting by the operand width or larger is undefined. Parse negative external indices before converting them to unsigned. XOR toggles a bit; it does not unconditionally set or clear it. Concurrent shared updates require atomic read-modify-write operations or other synchronization.

