**Key idea:** randomized startup phase is testbench stimulus. Keep every clock delay positive after time quantization and preserve reproducibility through the simulator seed.

```systemverilog
module random_phase_clock;
  timeunit 1ns;
  timeprecision 1ps;
  logic clk;
  int unsigned phase_ps;
  initial begin
    clk=0;
    phase_ps=$urandom_range(9999,0);
    #(phase_ps * 1ps);
    forever #5ns clk=~clk;
  end
  initial begin
    #200ns;
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

| Property | This example |
|---|---|
| Initial level | Known low |
| Random startup delay | 0..9999 ps |
| Subsequent half-period | 5 ns |
| Steady frequency | 100 MHz |

### Explanation
The initial delay varies the relationship to other stimulus or independently generated clocks. After startup, the period remains fixed. The first rising edge occurs after the randomized delay plus the first half-period. Record the simulator's seed and relevant sampled parameters so failures can be replayed.

### Pitfalls and follow-up
An uninitialized clock toggled as `~clk` remains X, so assign an initial value explicitly. If also randomizing period or duty cycle, ensure both high and low intervals remain positive and satisfy the test's specification. A random initial register value is a different experiment: reset should still establish legal functional state. Phase variation improves simulation coverage but cannot simulate analog metastability or replace structural CDC analysis.
